dbHarness proposes to leverage Deutsche Bank’s existing GPU infrastructure to make powerful open-weight AI models accessible and practical for internal engineering and enterprise workflows.

We will begin by deploying a capable open-weight coding model in collaboration with dbLLM and integrating it with an open-source agent harness such as Pi or OpenCode. The resulting agent will be evaluated on end-to-end software engineering tasks, including bug investigation, code modification, sandboxed testing, and pull-request generation.

We will benchmark accuracy, latency, developer effort, and cost against existing Gemini CLI and Copilot-assisted workflows, with a hypothetical Claude Code subscription scenario as an additional cost reference. The objective is to validate whether internally hosted coding agents can deliver competitive performance with favorable economics at scale.

Beyond coding, our ambition is to collaborate with dbLLM and Ada to make open-weight models a routinely accessible option across Deutsche Bank, enabling applications using OCR, embeddings, vision, and advanced reasoning models.

By running inference within approved bank-controlled infrastructure, we can keep sensitive code, prompts, and model outputs within internal security boundaries, reducing reliance on external AI services for suitable workloads while strengthening data control, cost efficiency, and internal AI engineering expertise.